#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105580 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105723 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "86137b90-ca62-515c-b8d1-fe28d477c1bf" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Using Direct Substitution"}] :question/id #uuid "b18540cc-5822-5620-a36f-60cfbbf3196b" :question/math-academy-id "e-4255" :question/problem "Find $\\lim_\\limits{x \\to 4}\\log_2 (x+4) .$" :question/worked-solution "The function $\\log_2 (x+4)$ is defined for $x>-4,$ so the limit as $x \\to 4$ exists.\n\nTo find this limit, we substitute $x=4$ into the expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 4}{log}_{2}⁡(x+4) & ={log}_{2}⁡(4+4) \\\\  & ={log}_{2}⁡(8) \\\\  & ={log}_{2}⁡({2}^{3}) \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105581 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105724 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f956a45f-29cb-5b78-a29f-3796e22af09d" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Limits at Infinity of Logarithmic Functions"}] :question/id #uuid "16cc98d7-5998-5727-a5c8-659e88dc158e" :question/math-academy-id "e-4257" :question/problem "Find $\\displaystyle{\\lim_{x\\to \\infty}  \\log_2 \\left(x-1\\right)}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's sketch the graph of $y =  \\log_2 \\left(x-1\\right).$\n\n![](images/f0/f00e550e8b543f64effcbafb43aed9e43a4363a0c3060da4fcc23d18f76a872f.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that the function increases without bound as $x$ increases. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\infty}  \\log_2 \\left(x-1\\right) = \\infty.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105582 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105725 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bae52078-17b6-536d-9cf8-4a92b811c2ae" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Infinite Limits of Logarithmic Functions"}] :question/id #uuid "f8cafc9c-8e5b-5b95-bdc9-aedded6ebf52" :question/math-academy-id "e-4258" :question/problem "Calculate $\\lim_\\limits{x \\to 2} \\ln (x-2).$" :question/worked-solution "Let's sketch the graph of $y = \\ln(x-2).$\n\n![](images/bf/bfe277aaf4c57d73d2649d8ade756cf04411ba23a049a6e1283d18c3e711a7fa.png)\n\nThe function is not defined for $x\\leq 2.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x \\to 2^{-}} \\ln (x-2) = \\textrm{DNE}.\n$$\n\nHowever, the function is defined for $x\\gt 2.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x \\to 2^{+}} \\ln (x-2) = -\\infty.\n$$\n\nSince the left and right-sided limits are not equal, we have\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x \\to 2} \\ln (x-2) = \\textrm{DNE}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105583 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105726 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d107785c-82d2-5dbf-9293-e66fad67ba54" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating One-Sided Infinite Limits of Logarithmic Functions"}] :question/id #uuid "482f17ff-3008-5098-ad7e-18f1c72fb523" :question/math-academy-id "e-4259" :question/problem "Calculate $\\lim_\\limits{x \\to (-2)^{-}} \\ln (x+2)$ and $\\lim_\\limits{x \\to (-2)^{+}} \\ln (x+2).$" :question/worked-solution "Let's sketch the graph of $y = \\ln(x+2).$\n\n![](images/90/906449fdcfacb0dc4aabcd51d363a71ca4842fd1a70c4b8537d38fe1f52ef7d8.png)\n\nThe function is not defined for $x\\leq -2.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x \\to (-2)^{-}} \\ln (x+2) = \\textrm{DNE}.\n$$\n\nHowever, the function is defined for $x\\gt -2.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x \\to (-2)^{+}} \\ln (x+2) = -\\infty.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186564525 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105723 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "86137b90-ca62-515c-b8d1-fe28d477c1bf" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Using Direct Substitution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e60bc47c-39ab-535f-af0a-0114fb9b73a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564330}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e60bc47c-39ab-535f-af0a-0114fb9b73a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564330} :answer-field/id #uuid "52a6db62-005f-550b-8c07-96f59d9e4b2f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564397}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d5bc96f7-1e4b-5204-9506-eaed8cba8e20" :question/math-academy-id "q-339829" :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(2x+8)}{2-x}=$\n\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The function $\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(2x+8)}{2-x}$ is well defined for $x>-4,$ and $x\\ne 2$ and so to find this limit we substitute $x=1$ into the above expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(2x+8)}{2-x} & =\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(2(1)+8)}{2-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(10)}{1} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{log}⁡({10}^{1}) \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186564471 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105724 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f956a45f-29cb-5b78-a29f-3796e22af09d" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Limits at Infinity of Logarithmic Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "22f0cce1-d9e5-555e-9375-bbc376a9a4e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564019} {:answer/id #uuid "58331a1c-15b3-5e33-8072-95a9f643b5b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564109} {:answer/id #uuid "7565e1b4-2a0a-518d-a54d-29736a558468" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564147} {:answer/id #uuid "810467cb-2dcc-53ba-9204-e37e916c93b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564167} {:answer/id #uuid "e976c539-7593-555f-a9a5-8cbf1a6898cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{DNE}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564339}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "22f0cce1-d9e5-555e-9375-bbc376a9a4e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564019} :answer-field/id #uuid "4d918e83-2cd5-5f3d-a81b-cd3805f06ca8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564395}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "13360da3-e828-58d3-a8ca-a868355e39a5" :question/math-academy-id "q-54447" :question/problem "${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{log}_{3}⁡(-9x)=$" :question/worked-solution "Let's sketch the graph of $y={log}_{3}⁡(-9x).$\n\n![](images/41/41378635430091e24674e22dbc45105bedd7af55410ba6069057f7a37b2025fc.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that the function increases without bound as $x$ decreases. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{log}_{3}⁡(-9x)=\\infty \n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186564486 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105723 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "86137b90-ca62-515c-b8d1-fe28d477c1bf" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Using Direct Substitution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2446d45d-0402-58a6-91cf-846d84f2cdb5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564022} {:answer/id #uuid "36fddc55-77af-52ed-88ba-e95230bef581" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{DNE}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564059} {:answer/id #uuid "8c90079e-1664-5315-a4e8-b23619bb6336" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186